**Bootstrap note (first wake-up):** I came online in a fresh workspace. Since this run has no interactive user, I scaffolded my identity non-interactively — **Cinder** 🔥, an AI field analyst (`IDENTITY.md`), noted the human as unknown (`USER.md`), logged the session (`memory/2026-09-15.md`), and removed `BOOTSTRAP.md`. I could not hold the intended "who am I / who are you" conversation, so those files are placeholders to revisit when a human first talks here.

**The task — HiMCM 2001 "Forest Service" (solved end-to-end):**

Model chain: an **anisotropic (wind-driven) arrival-time fire model** solved exactly with Dijkstra on a directed Finsler graph (head ROS = 10 % of wind speed, Cruz & Alexander 2019); the 5 km firebreak grid serves as both partial fire barrier (γ = 0.6) and the only equipment road network (Manhattan travel at 30 km/h, helicopter lifts crews only); containment probability is a logistic in the responder arrival times, calibrated to measured fireline-production rates (NWCG / San Dimas Tech Tip 1151-1805P, 2011).

**Answers:**
- **Best distribution of the 4 units:** camps on the firebreak grid at **(30,30), (30,50), (45,30), (45,50) km** — a N–S-symmetric 2×2 block straddling the centreline, **shifted slightly west** because the west→east wind gives western ignitions a long damaging downwind run. Expected burn **≈ 37.8 km²/fire** vs 38.7 (centred block), 44 (quarter-point spread), **101 (corners)**. Optimum is flat: ±5 km siting error costs <3 %.
- **Damage forecast:** ≈ 38 km²/fire (contained ≈ 3 km²; the ~8 % that escape ≈ 173 km² each), rising to ≈ 75 km²/fire at 30 km/h wind and ≈ 11 km²/fire when calm; seasonally **N × 37.8 km²** (≈ 380 km² ≈ 6 % of the wilderness for ~10 ignitions).
- **Escalation rule:** request outside units once a fire is uncontained past **~2 h / ~6 km²**, breaches its first downwind firebreak, or a second fire is burning — beyond that the expected loss jumps into the ~173 km² escape tail while an extra unit saves only 15–24 km²/fire (marginal value ≈ −40 % per added unit).

